    "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source workshop-facsimile\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-workshop-additions/ws-source-011.png\" width=\"909\" height=\"405\" alt=\"1. Sandlar Ltd (Sandlar) had distributable profits of £725,000 at 1 January 20X5. \nDuring the year ended 31 December 20X5 it made profits of £210,000 after \naccounting for the following: \n \n• An investment property held under the fair value model was remeasured \nfrom £89,000 to £100,000. \n• A dividend of £55,000 was paid. \n• A revalued building was sold for a gain of £30,000. The revaluation surplus \nrelating to the building at disposal was £90,000. \n• Land measured under the revaluation model was remeasured from \n£400,000 to £340,000. Immediately before the revaluation, the revaluation \nsurplus had a balance of £25,000 relating to the land. \n \nWhat are the distributable profits of Sandlar at 31 December 20X5?\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source workshop-facsimile\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-workshop-additions/ws-source-012.png\" width=\"909\" height=\"618\" alt=\"Q1 Workings: \n \n£000 \nDistributable profits at 1 Jan 20X5 \n725 \nAdd: Profit for the year \n210 \nLess: Unrealised gain on investment property \n(11) \nLess: Dividend \n(55) \nAdd: Realised revaluation surplus \n90 \nDistributable profits at 31 Dec 20X5 \n959 \n \nExplanations: \n Fair value gains on investment properties are unrealised. The gain is included \nin the profit for the year, which has been added into distributable profits. So \nthe gain needs to be deducted. \n Dividends are paid out of distributable profits and needs to be deducted. \n The building has been sold and the revaluation surplus is now a realised gain \nso it needs to be added to the distributable profits \n £25,000 of the downwards revaluation is recognised in OCI (and reduces the \nrevaluation surplus to nil). This is an unrealised loss; however, no adjustment \nis required because it was not recognised in profits. The balance of £35,000 of \nthe downwards revaluation is recognised in profit or loss as an impairment \nloss. This is realised and has already been included in profit or loss and \ntherefore no adjustment is required.\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "searchText": "1. Sandlar Ltd (Sandlar) had distributable profits of £725,000 at 1 January 20X5. \nDuring the year ended 31 December 20X5 it made profits of £210,000 after \naccounting for the following: \n \n• An investment property held under the fair value model was remeasured \nfrom £89,000 to £100,000. \n• A dividend of £55,000 was paid. \n• A revalued building was sold for a gain of £30,000. The revaluation surplus \nrelating to the building at disposal was £90,000. \n• Land measured under the revaluation model was remeasured from \n£400,000 to £340,000. Immediately before the revaluation, the revaluation \nsurplus had a balance of £25,000 relating to the land. \n \nWhat are the distributable profits of Sandlar at 31 December 20X5?",
