    "source": "Workshop 2 · Q2.2 Robot Electronics",
    "provider": "ICAS",
    "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source workshop-facsimile\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-workshop-additions/ws-source-015.png\" width=\"909\" height=\"567\" alt=\"2. The directors of Robot Electronics Ltd (Robot) have asked you to advise on the \nmaximum amount that the company could distribute as dividends at 31 \nDecember 20X6. \n \n• All retained earnings of £2,175,000 were distributable at 1 January 20X6.  \n• Robot reported a draft profit of £675,000 for the year ended 31 December \n20X6.  \n• Robot measures its property under the revaluation model. However, it \ndoes not make transfers from revaluation surplus to retained earnings in \nrespect of excess depreciation. At 31 December 20X6, accumulated \ndepreciation on the revalued assets was £332,000 and accumulated \ndepreciation would have been £115,000 on the historical cost basis.  \n• Included in the profit for the year is a gain of £25,000, representing the \nincrease in fair value of a derivative for which there is a readily available \nmarket.  \n• In March 20X6, Robot sold electrical goods to a customer in exchange for \nmachine tools. As the fair value of the machine tools exceeded the cost of \nthe electrical goods, a £22,000 gain was included in profit for the year \n• A supplier invoice in US dollars included in Trade Payables at 31 \nDecember 20X6 was retranslated at a gain of £25,000.\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source workshop-facsimile\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-workshop-additions/ws-source-013.png\" width=\"909\" height=\"572\" alt=\"Q2 Workings: \n \n£000 \nDistributable profits at 1 Jan 20X6 \n2,175 \nAdd: Profit for the year \n675 \nAdd: Excess depreciation on revalued assets (332k – 115k) \n217 \nLess: Unrealised gain on exchange \n(22) \n \n \nDistributable profits at 31 Dec 20X6 \n3,045 \n \nExplanations: \n The excess depreciation on a revalued asset is considered realised regardless \nof whether the company makes a transfer or not. The excess depreciation is \ntherefore added to the distributable profits. \n The £25k fair value gain for a derivative which has a readily available market \nis a realised gain. Derivatives are always measured at FVTPL so the gain is \nincluded within the profit figure which has already been added to the \ndistributable profits thus no additional amendment required. \n The £22k gain made on the non cash exchange is unrealised as the \nconsideration is not a qualifying consideration. As it is included within the profit \nfigure which has been added to distributable profits already, it needs to be \ndeducted.\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source workshop-facsimile\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-workshop-additions/ws-source-014.png\" width=\"909\" height=\"117\" alt=\" An exchange gain on the settlement of a monetary item or the retranslation of \na monetary item is a realised profit. The gain from the retranslation of the \ntrade payables figure will be included within the profit figure which has already \nbeen added to the distributable profits thus no additional amendment required.\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "searchText": "2. The directors of Robot Electronics Ltd (Robot) have asked you to advise on the \nmaximum amount that the company could distribute as dividends at 31 \nDecember 20X6. \n \n• All retained earnings of £2,175,000 were distributable at 1 January 20X6.  \n• Robot reported a draft profit of £675,000 for the year ended 31 December \n20X6.  \n• Robot measures its property under the revaluation model. However, it \ndoes not make transfers from revaluation surplus to retained earnings in \nrespect of excess depreciation. At 31 December 20X6, accumulated \ndepreciation on the revalued assets was £332,000 and accumulated \ndepreciation would have been £115,000 on the historical cost basis.  \n• Included in the profit for the year is a gain of £25,000, representing the \nincrease in fair value of a derivative for which there is a readily available \nmarket.  \n• In March 20X6, Robot sold electrical goods to a customer in exchange for \nmachine tools. As the fair value of the machine tools exceeded the cost of \nthe electrical goods, a £22,000 gain was included in profit for the year \n• A supplier invoice in US dollars included in Trade Payables at 31 \nDecember 20X6 was retranslated at a gain of £25,000.",
